.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1128 AM PST Sat Nov 7 2026
(This afternoon through Sunday)

Temperatures are forecast to be in the upper 60s to low 70s near
the coast with gradual warming as you move inland away from the
coast. Meanwhile, the interior valleys are forecast to reach into
the mid 70s to lower 80s across the interior Central Coast as
sunny skies prevail. This is some 5-10 deg F above normal. Gentle
to moderate offshore flow will persist through the night in the
higher elevations before diminishing by Sunday afternoon. All of
this is ahead of a cold front that is forecast to push inland
across Northern California by Sunday morning and potentially
making its way into the North Bay by the afternoon and early
evening hours. However, as the front weakens, no measurable
rainfall is expected across the North Bay. Temperatures on Sunday
will be similar to those this afternoon, yet the incoming front
passing to our north may result in increased cloud cover across
the Bay Area and Central Coast.

Got beach plans? Check out the BEACHES section of the AFD for
safety tips and more information on the Beach Hazards Statement
and Coastal Flood Advisory.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1128 AM PST Sat Nov 7 2026
(Sunday night through next Friday)

Not much has changed in the extended forecast. High pressure will
rebuild in wake of the exiting trough across the Pacific
Northwest from Tuesday through Thursday, maintaining afternoon
temperatures up to 10-15 deg F above early-November climatological
norms by Wednesday. Mid-to-upper level troughing remains forecast
to deepen as it approaches the West Coast as early as Thursday.
This is being depicted by ensemble models and clusters late next
week and into the following weekend. However, their remains a
great deal of uncertainty regarding the exact track of this system
and its available moisture source. For now, enjoy the warm
weather that is forecast to persist through midweek, just be
mindful of the coastal hazards! Also, check back for next week`s
rain chances and their impacts.

